@meta
id: 08-16
title: D and C take the rest of the line
chapter: 08
type: lesson
skills: delete-d, change-c
par: 37
minutes: 5
vscode: click, Shift-End, Delete
open: payment.service.ts

@brief
"From here to the end of the line" is the most common thing you ever ask
an editor to do — it is the Shift-End of your old life. Vim gives it to
you as a single capital letter, twice: one that deletes and one that
changes.

Two jobs in this service.

The idempotency key has a stale TODO comment hanging off the end of it.
The comment goes; the code before it stays.

The gateway call still passes `mode: 'legacy'`, which the new gateway
rejects. The call should end up as:

    return this.gateway.post('/refunds', { orderId, key });

@keys
| `D` | delete from the cursor to the end of the line |
| `C` | change from the cursor to the end of the line |
| `f{` | forward to the next `{` |
| `f/` | forward to the next `/` |

@hints
Both edits are "everything from this point onwards, on this line". Land
on the first character you want gone, then say so once.
--
`D` deletes to the end of the line and stays in normal mode. `C` does the
same and leaves you in insert mode to type the replacement.
--
On the comment line, `f/` lands on the first slash — `D` from there. On
the gateway line, `f{` lands on the opening brace of the argument, and
`C` lets you retype the tail from there.

@solution
    2j f/ D  j f{ C{ orderId, key });<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

`D` leaves a trailing space behind where the comment was. Nobody can see
it, the grader ignores it, and your formatter will strip it — but if it
bothers you, land one character earlier with `h` before pressing `D`.

Both of these are shorthand. `D` is `d$` and `C` is `c$`, spelled with
one key because you will want them hundreds of times a day. Vim is full
of this: the operations people actually repeat get a capital letter of
their own.

Which makes `Y` the odd one out. By the pattern it should be `y$`, and it
is not — `Y` yanks the whole line, the same as `yy`. There is no
single-key "yank to the end of the line". It is the one inconsistency in
this family and it catches everybody once.

@start payment.service.ts
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const key = `refund:${orderId}`; // TODO drop when the gateway is fixed
    return this.gateway.post('/refunds', { orderId, key, mode: 'legacy' });
  }
}

@expected payment.service.ts
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const key = `refund:${orderId}`;
    return this.gateway.post('/refunds', { orderId, key });
  }
}
